Smart Home Automation using ESP32 — Project Plan
1) Project Overview

Goal: Control home appliances (e.g., lamp, fan) using an ESP32 via Wi-Fi using MQTT. Provide local control (buttons) and remote control via a Node-RED dashboard (web/mobile). Log sensor data (temperature/humidity) and show live values.

Key features:
>Control multiple relays (ON/OFF) via MQTT topics.
>Local push-button interrupts for manual control.
>Publish sensor telemetry (DHT22) periodically.
>Node-RED dashboard to control & monitor devices.
>Optionally use secured MQTT (username/password).

2) Bill of Materials (BOM)
ESP32 development board (e.g., ESP32-DevKitC) — 1
4-channel Relay module (5V) — 1 (or as many channels as devices)
DHT22 or DHT11 temperature/humidity sensor — 1
Push buttons (momentary) — 2 (for local control)
10kΩ resistors (for pull-downs/pull-ups) — 2
Jumper wires, breadboard
USB cable for ESP32
(Optional) 5V power supply for relays (if using external)
MQTT broker: Mosquitto server (local PC/RPi or cloud) or free broker (test.mosquitto.org)

3) System Architecture (high level)
[Mobile/Web Browser] <--HTTP--> [Node-RED Dashboard]
      ↑                               ↑
      | (MQTT publish/sub)            | (MQTT connect)
      |                               |
  Internet/Wi-Fi -------------------- MQTT Broker (Mosquitto)
                                       |
                                       |
                                   ESP32 (client)
                                   - Subscribes to control topics
                                   - Publishes telemetry topics
                                   - Reads buttons via interrupts
                                   - Drives relays (GPIO)
                                   - Reads DHT sensor



4) MQTT Topic Scheme (recommended)
Use a predictable topic structure:
home/<room>/device/<id>/set — payload: "ON" / "OFF" (commands to ESP32)
home/<room>/device/<id>/state — payload: "ON" / "OFF" (ESP32 publishes current state)
home/<room>/sensor/temperature — payload: numeric (°C)
home/<room>/sensor/humidity — payload: numeric (%)
home/esp32/online — payload: "true" / "false" (last-will/online status)
Example:
home/livingroom/device/1/set
home/livingroom/device/1/state
home/livingroom/sensor/temperature


5) Wiring & Pin Assignments (example)
Assume ESP32 DevKit, change pins if needed.
Relay IN1 -> GPIO 16
Relay IN2 -> GPIO 17
Relay VCC -> 5V (module) (or 3.3V depending on module)
Relay GND -> GND
DHT22 DATA -> GPIO 4 (with pull-up on DATA line)
DHT22 VCC -> 3.3V
DHT22 GND -> GND
Button1 -> GPIO 0 (one side to GPIO, other to GND; use internal pull-up)
Button2 -> GPIO 2
ESP32 GND -> Relay GND (common ground)





6) Firmware — Complete Arduino (C/C++) Code
/*
  esp32_smart_home.ino
  Smart Home Automation (ESP32) using MQTT + DHT22 + Relays + Buttons (interrupts)
  Requirements:
    - Arduino core for ESP32 installed
    - PubSubClient library
    - DHT sensor library (Adafruit or equivalent)
*/

#include <WiFi.h>
#include <PubSubClient.h>
#include "DHT.h"

// ========== CONFIG ==========
const char* ssid = "YOUR_WIFI_SSID";
const char* password = "YOUR_WIFI_PASSWORD";

const char* mqtt_server = "BROKER_IP_OR_HOST"; // e.g., "192.168.1.10" or "broker.hivemq.com"
const int mqtt_port = 1883;
const char* mqtt_user = ""; // set if used
const char* mqtt_pass = ""; // set if used

const char* baseTopic = "home/livingroom"; // change room as needed
const char* deviceId = "device1";

#define DHTPIN 4
#define DHTTYPE DHT22

// GPIO for relays
const int RELAY1_PIN = 16;
const int RELAY2_PIN = 17;

// Buttons (use internal pullup)
const int BUTTON1_PIN = 0;
const int BUTTON2_PIN = 2;

// Debounce
volatile unsigned long lastInterruptTime1 = 0;
volatile unsigned long lastInterruptTime2 = 0;

DHT dht(DHTPIN, DHTTYPE);

WiFiClient espClient;
PubSubClient client(espClient);

// Forward declarations
void connectWiFi();
void connectMQTT();
void mqttCallback(char* topic, byte* payload, unsigned int length);
void publishState(int relayPin, const char* topicState);
void IRAM_ATTR handleButton1();
void IRAM_ATTR handleButton2();

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  delay(1000);
  pinMode(RELAY1_PIN, OUTPUT);
  pinMode(RELAY2_PIN, OUTPUT);
  digitalWrite(RELAY1_PIN, LOW); // assuming LOW = OFF, set based on relay logic
  digitalWrite(RELAY2_PIN, LOW);

  pinMode(BUTTON1_PIN, INPUT_PULLUP);
  pinMode(BUTTON2_PIN, INPUT_PULLUP);

  // Attach interrupts: FALLING (button press to ground)
  attachInterrupt(BUTTON1_PIN, handleButton1, FALLING);
  attachInterrupt(BUTTON2_PIN, handleButton2, FALLING);

  dht.begin();

  connectWiFi();
  client.setServer(mqtt_server, mqtt_port);
  client.setCallback(mqttCallback);

  // Last Will testament
  String lastWillTopic = String(baseTopic) + "/esp32/online";
  client.disconnect();
  // PubSubClient doesn't set Last Will easily; instead publish online status after connect
}

void loop() {
  if (!client.connected()) {
    connectMQTT();
  }
  client.loop();

  static unsigned long lastTelem = 0;
  unsigned long now = millis();
  if (now - lastTelem > 15000) { // every 15 seconds
    lastTelem = now;
    float t = dht.readTemperature();
    float h = dht.readHumidity();
    if (!isnan(t) && !isnan(h)) {
      char buf[32];
      snprintf(buf, sizeof(buf), "%.2f", t);
      String topicT = String(baseTopic) + "/sensor/temperature";
      client.publish(topicT.c_str(), buf, true);

      snprintf(buf, sizeof(buf), "%.2f", h);
      String topicH = String(baseTopic) + "/sensor/humidity";
      client.publish(topicH.c_str(), buf, true);

      Serial.printf("Published Temp: %s, Humidity: %s\n", topicT.c_str(), topicH.c_str());
    } else {
      Serial.println("Failed to read DHT sensor");
    }
  }
}

// ===== Functions =====
void connectWiFi() {
  Serial.printf("Connecting to WiFi: %s\n", ssid);
  WiFi.begin(ssid, password);
  int tries = 0;
  while (WiFi.status() != WL_CONNECTED && tries < 30) {
    delay(500);
    Serial.print(".");
    tries++;
  }
  if (WiFi.status() == WL_CONNECTED) {
    Serial.println("\nWiFi connected");
    Serial.print("IP: ");
    Serial.println(WiFi.localIP());
  } else {
    Serial.println("\nFailed to connect to WiFi");
  }
}

void connectMQTT() {
  while (!client.connected()) {
    Serial.print("Attempting MQTT connection...");
    String clientId = "esp32-" + String(WiFi.macAddress());
    bool ok;
    if (strlen(mqtt_user) > 0) {
      ok = client.connect(clientId.c_str(), mqtt_user, mqtt_pass, (String(baseTopic)+"/esp32/online").c_str(), 0, true, "false");
    } else {
      ok = client.connect(clientId.c_str());
    }
    if (ok) {
      Serial.println("connected");
      // Publish online status
      String topicOnline = String(baseTopic) + "/esp32/online";
      client.publish(topicOnline.c_str(), "true", true);
      // Subscribe to control topics
      String t1 = String(baseTopic) + "/device/1/set";
      String t2 = String(baseTopic) + "/device/2/set";
      client.subscribe(t1.c_str());
      client.subscribe(t2.c_str());
      // Request retained state if needed
    } else {
      Serial.print("failed, rc=");
      Serial.print(client.state());
      Serial.println(" try again in 5s");
      delay(5000);
    }
  }
}

void mqttCallback(char* topic, byte* payload, unsigned int length) {
  String msg;
  for (unsigned int i = 0; i < length; i++) msg += (char)payload[i];
  Serial.printf("Message arrived [%s]: %s\n", topic, msg.c_str());

  String sTopic = String(topic);
  if (sTopic.endsWith("/device/1/set")) {
    if (msg.equalsIgnoreCase("ON")) {
      digitalWrite(RELAY1_PIN, HIGH);
      publishState(RELAY1_PIN, (String(baseTopic) + "/device/1/state").c_str());
    } else if (msg.equalsIgnoreCase("OFF")) {
      digitalWrite(RELAY1_PIN, LOW);
      publishState(RELAY1_PIN, (String(baseTopic) + "/device/1/state").c_str());
    }
  } else if (sTopic.endsWith("/device/2/set")) {
    if (msg.equalsIgnoreCase("ON")) {
      digitalWrite(RELAY2_PIN, HIGH);
      publishState(RELAY2_PIN, (String(baseTopic) + "/device/2/state").c_str());
    } else if (msg.equalsIgnoreCase("OFF")) {
      digitalWrite(RELAY2_PIN, LOW);
      publishState(RELAY2_PIN, (String(baseTopic) + "/device/2/state").c_str());
    }
  }
}

void publishState(int relayPin, const char* topicState) {
  const char* state = (digitalRead(relayPin) == HIGH) ? "ON" : "OFF";
  client.publish(topicState, state, true);
  Serial.printf("Published state %s to %s\n", state, topicState);
}

// ===== Interrupt handlers =====
void IRAM_ATTR handleButton1() {
  unsigned long t = millis();
  if (t - lastInterruptTime1 > 200) {
    // toggle relay1
    int cur = digitalRead(RELAY1_PIN);
    digitalWrite(RELAY1_PIN, !cur);
    // publish new state - note: avoid heavy operations inside ISR; flag system in real use
    String topic = String(baseTopic) + "/device/1/state";
    if (client.connected()) {
      client.publish(topic.c_str(), (digitalRead(RELAY1_PIN) ? "ON" : "OFF"), true);
    }
    lastInterruptTime1 = t;
  }
}

void IRAM_ATTR handleButton2() {
  unsigned long t = millis();
  if (t - lastInterruptTime2 > 200) {
    int cur = digitalRead(RELAY2_PIN);
    digitalWrite(RELAY2_PIN, !cur);
    String topic = String(baseTopic) + "/device/2/state";
    if (client.connected()) {
      client.publish(topic.c_str(), (digitalRead(RELAY2_PIN) ? "ON" : "OFF"), true);
    }
    lastInterruptTime2 = t;
  }
}



Notes about the code
Change ssid, password, mqtt_server accordingly.
The code uses interrupts for buttons — ISR toggles relays and publishes state. For production, better to set flags in ISR and handle MQTT publish in loop to avoid heavy ISR work.
digitalWrite HIGH/LOW mapping depends on your relay module (active LOW vs HIGH). Test and invert if necessary.
Add MQTT username/password if your broker requires auth.


7) Setting up MQTT Broker (Mosquitto) — Quickstart (on PC/RPi)
Linux (Ubuntu / RPi):
sudo apt update
sudo apt install mosquitto mosquitto-clients -y
sudo systemctl enable mosquitto
sudo systemctl start mosquitto
# Test publish/subscribe
mosquitto_sub -h localhost -t "home/livingroom/#" -v
mosquitto_pub -h localhost -t "home/livingroom/device/1/set" -m "ON"


8) Node-RED Dashboard Setup (simple)
Install Node-RED (on PC or Raspberry Pi).
sudo npm i -g --unsafe-perm node-red
Start: node-red
Open http://localhost:1880 in browser.
Install node-red-dashboard and node-red-contrib-mqtt-broker if needed via Manage Palette.
Flow outline:
MQTT in node subscribing to home/livingroom/sensor/# → gauge/text nodes to display temp/humidity.
UI Button nodes → MQTT out to home/livingroom/device/1/set with payload "ON"/"OFF".
MQTT in for home/livingroom/device/1/state → UI LED node (or text) to show current device state.
You can create a dashboard at http://<node-red-host>:1880/ui.


9) Testing Checklist
Flash firmware to ESP32; watch Serial Monitor for IP and MQTT connection messages.
On successful connection, check that home/livingroom/esp32/online published "true".
Use mosquitto_sub or MQTT Explorer to subscribe to home/livingroom/# and watch messages.
Press local buttons → ensure relays toggle and state messages published.
From Node-RED dashboard, press UI buttons → ensure ESP32 receives set messages and toggles relays.
Verify telemetry (temperature/humidity) appears every 15 seconds.

10) Debugging Tips & Common Issues
No Wi-Fi: Check SSID/password, WiFi channel (2.4 GHz only), router MAC filtering.
MQTT fail to connect: Verify broker host/port, firewall, and credentials.
Relays not switching: Check wiring, common ground, and whether relay is active LOW (inverted).
DHT returns NaN: Check wiring, correct pin, and timing (DHT needs single digital pin).
ISR causing crashes: Keep ISR minimal. Set flags in ISR and process in loop().


11) Security & Production Notes
Use TLS (MQTTS) and broker authentication in real deployment.
Avoid using attachInterrupt heavy logic — use flagging pattern for reliability.
For multiple devices and scale, consider using MQTT topic namespaces and retained messages for state persistence.
